[SIZE=6][B]A technical exercise: using location population structure to achieve stratum-level control[/B][/SIZE]

[I]A technical write-up on the country-level demand solver in Standard of Living (SOL), a mod for EU5. If you get something out of reading this, please consider supporting [URL='https://forum.paradoxplaza.com/forum/threads/sol-standard-of-living.1501680/']my mod thread[/URL] — since PDX will not restore the interface, I have had to spend a great deal of time and effort rebuilding the mod's core functionality.[/I]

[HR][/HR]

[SIZE=5][B]Contents[/B][/SIZE]

[LIST=1]
[*]The bottleneck
[*]The strategy
[*]The algorithm
[*]Solver outcomes
[*]Performance
[*]Conclusion
[/LIST]

[HR][/HR]

[SIZE=5][B]1. The bottleneck[/B][/SIZE]

In the earliest SOL design, population demand was resolved per POP, which in principle allows the most precise control possible. That is why SOL 1.1 pushed as far as Engel curves (demand for different goods scaling differently with wealth) and substitution groups (consumers switching away from a good when it becomes expensive) — quite close to a Vic3-style demand model.

Then EU's 1.2 update removed direct pop access. I take this to have been a performance decision: cultural and religious effects on demand were hardcoded instead, so the engine no longer needs to walk script values pop by pop at runtime. But 1.2 thereby destroyed any possibility of dynamically steering demand during play, and with it SOL stopped working entirely.

A static-demand design does not fit an economic model at all, and PDX plainly did not intend that outcome, so 1.3 introduced a location-level interface: [ICODE]local_pop_demand[/ICODE]. It is a scalar — a plain number like 1.2 or 0.8 — that adjusts demand for every stratum in a location uniformly.

That interface is the only reason I could rebuild SOL for 1.3. But with no stratum resolution, I had to run the "income = expenditure" calculation on location-level aggregates rather than the per-stratum aggregates the original design used, and that creates a serious problem.

Set [ICODE]local_pop_demand = 1.2[/ICODE] on a location and every stratum's demand there is multiplied by 1.2:

[LIST]
[*]Noble silk demand x 1.2
[*]Clergy book demand x 1.2
[*]Burgher tool demand x 1.2
[*]Peasant grain demand x 1.2
[/LIST]

But because the strata differ in political power — above all through peasant autonomy — four strata in one location can be in wildly different positions:

[TABLE]
[TR][TH]Stratum[/TH][TH]Monthly income per capita[/TH][TH]Affordable baseline multiple[/TH][/TR]
[TR][TD]Nobles[/TD][TD]50[/TD][TD]2.0[/TD][/TR]
[TR][TD]Clergy[/TD][TD]20[/TD][TD]1.4[/TD][/TR]
[TR][TD]Burghers[/TD][TD]15[/TD][TD]1.1[/TD][/TR]
[TR][TD]Lower[/TD][TD]3[/TD][TD]0.4[/TD][/TR]
[/TABLE]

So: what value do you put on this location? Set 1.1 (roughly the weighted average) and nobles, who should be at 2.0, get 1.1 — 45% short; lower, who should be at 0.4, get 1.1 — 175% over. Whatever number you choose, you are simultaneously shortchanging some strata and overfunding others. Mathematically, one number cannot express four different demand coefficients.

[CENTER][IMG]FIGURE_1_URL[/IMG]
[SIZE=2][I]Figure 1: once the four strata's target multipliers (Nobles / Clergy / Burghers / Lower) are compressed into a single scalar, every stratum sits away from where it should be. Labels on the right are the relative error.[/I][/SIZE][/CENTER]

Under those conditions SOL's arithmetic cannot help but be wrong. My first attempt was to set the initial pop demand proportionally to a typical country's stratum structure (this is the per-1000-population demand you see when browsing goods in game), so that the four strata's spending roughly equalled their income. The error involved turned out to be far worse than my optimistic estimate:

[LIST=1]
[*]The gap between noble and commoner demand versus target is severe. Many players reported one of them spending several times, even dozens of times, its income. In serfdom countries especially, peasant autonomy is so low that commoner income is often under 5% of location income while commoners hold over half the baseline spending.
[*]Once a stratum starts accumulating savings, the SOL algorithm reads the growing balance as "high savings pressure" and expands demand for all strata. That pushes the already-overextended strata further into bankruptcy, while the demand conjured out of nothing feeds back into strengthening the national economy, letting the wealthy stratum save even more.
[/LIST]

In the 260814 update I applied three direct fixes:

[LIST=1]
[*]Re-enabled the EU5 engine feature that automatically reduces demand for a poor stratum, up to 50% off. This had been deliberately disabled to preserve the "income = expenditure" model, but it is in fact very helpful for stratum differentiation.
[*]Re-enabled the engine feature that automatically reduces demand when a good's price is too high, up to 100%. Combined with SOL controlling demand volume from base spending, this partially restores goods substitution, though without any notion of "groups". Also previously disabled for the same reason.
[*]Capped savings pressure. This correction had always used a -25% floor with no ceiling; for the reasons above, a ceiling has become necessary in the current version.
[/LIST]

After these three fixes the extreme imbalance in stratum spending is much reduced, but not solved. In a 1743 observer save, France (leaning freeholder) originally had noble spending at roughly 5x income and peasant spending at roughly 0.5x. After the fixes noble spending fell to around 4x.

[HR][/HR]

[SIZE=5][B]2. The strategy[/B][/SIZE]

Before the solution, I want to reconstruct how this approach surfaced — because the derivation itself contains an assumption that data later disproved, and understanding exactly where it failed is the key to the whole system.

A few days ago I realised something: stratum ratios differ enormously between locations. If so, we could in principle apply extra corrections per location to covertly emulate control over stratum demand.

Take nobles and peasants.

Location A is a remote village: low control, very few nobles, low peasant autonomy. Nobles are rich and peasants poor, so the computation might give nobles +500% and peasants -50%, with a weighted average of -40%. That number overestimates peasant demand and underestimates noble demand. If every location looked like A, peasants would stay permanently poor while nobles hoarded gold.

Location B is the capital: high control, a larger noble share, higher peasant autonomy too. Nobles are less rich and peasants less poor — perhaps nobles +20%, peasants +50%, averaging +25%. That reverses the bias, underestimating peasant demand and overestimating noble demand. A country of nothing but B would see peasants accumulating savings and nobles growing poor, just less dramatically than A.

[SIZE=2][I](Aside: these two figures are internally consistent. Working backwards from the average, nobles account for just 1.8% of base spending in A and 83.3% in B, matching "very few nobles" and "a larger noble share" respectively. That 1.8% spending share in the village, at roughly 10x peasant per-capita spending, is about 0.2% of the population.)[/I][/SIZE]

Suppose a country has one B and many A: peasants still end up poor and nobles still end up rich. Invert it — a more urbanised country with more freemen — and nobles end up poor instead. So the severity of the problem actually depends on the "power distribution" across locations and the relative counts of each location type. It is not a constant bias; it varies with a country's internal structure.

The key idea follows from that: what if we intervene once more, on top of the averaged result? Apply a demand bonus on noble-heavy B and a demand malus on noble-sparse A, and that neatly raises the effective total noble demand; reverse it and peasant demand rises instead. As long as a country holds both A-like and B-like locations, rebalancing two strata's spending becomes possible.

Thinking further, what this amounts to is changing "location-level income = expenditure" into "country-level income = expenditure".

The original approach should in theory compute, per location, each stratum's liquid funds over its base spending (the former depending on political power, the latter equalling demand x goods price x headcount). But with no stratum resolution it degenerates into total liquid funds over total base spending.

The new approach instead aggregates income across locations first, then solves for n location coefficients (n = location count) such that those coefficients, multiplied by local base spending and summed, land exactly on the country-level per-stratum income totals.

That gives only 4 equations (5 with tribesmen), against very high degrees of freedom — any two locations with linearly independent stratum base-spending vectors add another one.

My initial judgement was:

[QUOTE]Extracting as few as 4 or 5 linearly independent locations should be enough to solve for a set of coefficients, finally achieving genuinely stratum-resolved demand adjustment.[/QUOTE]

That judgement was half right: location heterogeneity really can reconstruct the stratum signal, and that is the foundation of the entire system. The wrong half is the feasibility criterion — linear independence guarantees the solution is unique, but the engine does not accept negative modifiers (a location's pops cannot run in reverse and produce goods), so what we need is a [B]nonnegative[/B] solution. Those are entirely different things. As we will see, because most countries' raw location-level corrections are wildly off across strata and tend to demand large extra coefficients, the solution usually carries a negative component and is completely unusable even when every solvability condition on the equations holds.

But the idea itself works: even without an exact solution we can approximate, and even a 1% improvement beats having no stratum resolution at all. So the real difficulty is this: how to maximise exact solving, how to extend approximate solving as broadly as possible, and how to keep performance within budget.

Let us check the arithmetic. Take three location types (data from real saves), 1000 population each, base spending proportional to population, with modifiers set to 1.8 / 1.2 / 0.7:

[TABLE]
[TR][TH]Location type[/TH][TH]Nobles[/TH][TH]Clergy[/TH][TH]Burghers[/TH][TH]Lower[/TH][TH]modifier[/TH][/TR]
[TR][TD]Manor belt[/TD][TD]40%[/TD][TD]15%[/TD][TD]10%[/TD][TD]35%[/TD][TD]1.8[/TD][/TR]
[TR][TD]Trade hub[/TD][TD]15%[/TD][TD]10%[/TD][TD]45%[/TD][TD]30%[/TD][TD]1.2[/TD][/TR]
[TR][TD]Farm province[/TD][TD]10%[/TD][TD]5%[/TD][TD]10%[/TD][TD]75%[/TD][TD]0.7[/TD][/TR]
[/TABLE]

National totals:

[TABLE]
[TR][TH]Stratum[/TH][TH]Uncorrected[/TH][TH]Corrected[/TH][TH]Effective multiplier[/TH][/TR]
[TR][TD]Nobles[/TD][TD]650[/TD][TD]970[/TD][TD]+49%[/TD][/TR]
[TR][TD]Clergy[/TD][TD]300[/TD][TD]425[/TD][TD]+42%[/TD][/TR]
[TR][TD]Burghers[/TD][TD]650[/TD][TD]790[/TD][TD]+22%[/TD][/TR]
[TR][TD]Lower[/TD][TD]1400[/TD][TD]1515[/TD][TD]+8%[/TD][/TR]
[/TABLE]

[CENTER][IMG]FIGURE_2_URL[/IMG]
[SIZE=2][I]Figure 2: left, the population composition of the three location types with each one's assigned modifier below the axis. Right, the four resulting nationwide effective multipliers; in-bar text is base spending to corrected spending.[/I][/SIZE][/CENTER]

This example shows several features of the strategy clearly:

[LIST=1]
[*]Three modifiers produced four different effective multipliers. The difference comes from weighting: 61.5% of nobles sit in the manor belt at 1.8, while 53.6% of lower sit in the farm province at 0.7.
[*]Note that 50% of clergy and 61.5% of nobles are both in the manor belt — raising nobles by weighting the manor belt cannot avoid dragging clergy up too, which is exactly why the table shows nobles +49% alongside clergy +42%. If a target demands nobles rise sharply while clergy stays put, this population structure cannot deliver it.
[*]More locations, better results: more available classes means a richer set of reachable combinations, and a better chance the target falls inside range. Conversely, a small country with only a handful of locations cannot assemble enough degrees of freedom no matter how it classifies them.
[/LIST]

How large is the stratum-structure difference in real saves? Across three saves, measuring each country's own locations for noble population share and taking the ratio of highest to lowest (counting only countries with 5+ locations):

[TABLE]
[TR][TH]Save[/TH][TH]Countries[/TH][TH]Noble share spread[/TH][TH]Max/min ratio[/TH][TH]All four covered[/TH][/TR]
[TR][TD]1337.10[/TD][TD]487[/TD][TD]0.0069[/TD][TD]3.8[/TD][TD]193 (39.6%)[/TD][/TR]
[TR][TD]1386.03[/TD][TD]430[/TD][TD]0.0085[/TD][TD]4.6[/TD][TD]256 (59.5%)[/TD][/TR]
[TR][TD]1743.04[/TD][TD]329[/TD][TD]0.0128[/TD][TD]5.8[/TD][TD]171 (52.0%)[/TD][/TR]
[/TABLE]

"All four covered" means every one of the four strata has some location where it is the most over-represented relative to the national mean — satisfied by 40% to 60% of countries.

A median ratio of 3.8 to 5.8 means that within a single country, the noble-densest location runs four to six times denser than the sparsest. The heterogeneity is real, and it rises as the campaign progresses.

Bucketed by country size the trend is sharper still:

[TABLE]
[TR][TH]Locations[/TH][TH]1337.10[/TH][TH]1386.03[/TH][TH]1743.04[/TH][/TR]
[TR][TD]5-9[/TD][TD]2.7[/TD][TD]2.9[/TD][TD]4.3[/TD][/TR]
[TR][TD]10-49[/TD][TD]4.5[/TD][TD]5.0[/TD][TD]6.0[/TD][/TR]
[TR][TD]50-199[/TD][TD]6.7[/TD][TD]9.0[/TD][TD]7.3[/TD][/TR]
[TR][TD]200+[/TD][TD]7.2[/TD][TD]7.5[/TD][TD]13.2[/TD][/TR]
[/TABLE]

Large countries have more classes and far greater internal variation than small ones, so their classes are more sharply differentiated.

[HR][/HR]

[SIZE=5][B]3. The algorithm[/B][/SIZE]

[I]This section goes into more technical detail. If that does not interest you, skip ahead to section 4.[/I]

[SPOILER="Section 3: notation, the linear system, exact solving, approximation, prefilters"]

[B]3.1 Notation[/B]

A country owns [ICODE]n[/ICODE] locations, partitioned into [ICODE]K[/ICODE] classes [ICODE]C_1 ... C_K[/ICODE].

Stratum set: [ICODE]S = {nobles, clergy, burghers, lower}[/ICODE], four of them.

Known quantities, computed each month in the location pulse:

[LIST]
[*][ICODE]I_s(l)[/ICODE] — net income (liquid funds) of stratum [ICODE]s[/ICODE] at location [ICODE]l[/ICODE]
[*][ICODE]B_s(l)[/ICODE] — base spending of stratum [ICODE]s[/ICODE] at location [ICODE]l[/ICODE]
[*][ICODE]m_raw(l)[/ICODE] — the raw demand coefficient, i.e. the income-weighted average when classes are ignored
[/LIST]

[CODE]m_raw(l) = ( sum over s of I_s(l) ) / ( sum over s of B_s(l) )[/CODE]

Country-level targets:

[CODE]t_s = sum over all locations l of I_s(l)          for each stratum s[/CODE]

That is, each stratum's total liquid funds nationwide.

System matrix [ICODE]M[/ICODE], a 4 x K matrix:

[CODE]M[s][k] = sum over locations l in class C_k of ( m_raw(l) * B_s(l) )[/CODE]

Element [ICODE](s,k)[/ICODE] is the raw-weighted base spending of stratum [ICODE]s[/ICODE] summed over class [ICODE]k[/ICODE].

Class correction factors: the vector [ICODE]x = (x_1, ..., x_K)[/ICODE].

[B]3.2 The linear system[/B]

The equation to solve:

[CODE]M x = t        subject to    x >= 0[/CODE]

where [ICODE]t = (t_nobles, t_clergy, t_burghers, t_lower)[/ICODE].

The two parts mean:

[LIST]
[*][ICODE]M x = t[/ICODE] — each stratum's nationwide total demand equals its liquid funds
[*][ICODE]x >= 0[/ICODE] — modifiers cannot be negative (hard engine constraint)
[/LIST]

This is a constrained linear system. Drop the nonnegativity and a solution always exists as long as the matrix has full rank. Add [ICODE]x >= 0[/ICODE] and many systems become infeasible.

[B]3.3 Exact solving[/B]

The system has a solution exactly when the target vector [ICODE]t[/ICODE] lies inside the nonnegative cone spanned by [ICODE]M[/ICODE]'s columns.

The nonnegative cone is defined as:

[CODE]cone(M) = { sum over k of ( lambda_k * M[:,k] )  :  every lambda_k >= 0 }[/CODE]

where [ICODE]M[:,k][/ICODE] is the k-th column.

For the population structure from section 2, the three class columns are (ordered nobles / clergy / burghers / lower, with m_raw = 1):

[CODE]           Manor      Hub       Farm
nobles       400        150       100
clergy       150        100        50
burghers     100        450       100
lower        350        300       750[/CODE]

The nonnegative cone is every point reachable as [ICODE]x1*Manor + x2*Hub + x3*Farm[/ICODE] with all [ICODE]x >= 0[/ICODE]. If [ICODE]t[/ICODE] is in there, feasible; if not, infeasible.

Let the target be nobles +49% and lower -20%, with clergy and burghers unchanged, i.e. [ICODE]t = (968, 300, 650, 1120)[/ICODE].

Two rows suffice. The clergy-to-nobles ratio per column is [ICODE]150/400 = 0.375[/ICODE], [ICODE]100/150 = 0.667[/ICODE], [ICODE]50/100 = 0.500[/ICODE], while the target demands [ICODE]300/968 = 0.310[/ICODE]. Any nonnegative combination has a ratio bounded between 0.375 and 0.667, so 0.310 is unreachable — because 50% of clergy and 61.5% of nobles sit in the same manor belt, and raising nobles necessarily raises clergy with them.

That is the rigorous meaning of "outside the cone". Note how sensitive it is to the target: change it to clergy +40% (ratio 0.434, inside the band) and the maximum relative error under the same population structure immediately drops to 2.7% — nearly feasible. Whether a target is reachable depends on whether it respects the co-location relationships in the population structure, not on algorithmic precision.

Because the nonnegative cone is a narrow region — three or four nonnegative vectors span only a small slice of 4-dimensional space — the exact solution is infeasible in most cases. And [ICODE]t[/ICODE] is determined by actual income, which evolves independently of population structure, so it can easily land outside.

Furthermore, since each class column is a nonnegative sum of location columns:

[CODE]cone( any partition ) is a subset of cone( one column per location )[/CODE]

If even one independent coefficient per location is infeasible, no classification scheme can be feasible. That yields a fast test: treat each location as its own class, run one nonnegative least squares, and if that is infeasible then every classification is too.

[CENTER][IMG]FIGURE_3_URL[/IMG]
[SIZE=2][I]Figure 3: the four-dimensional system projected onto the (nobles, clergy) plane. Left: the three arrows are the class column vectors (Manor belt / Trade hub / Farm province); the cyan wedge is everything their nonnegative combinations can reach. The raw baseline sits inside the wedge, while the target (red X) has slope 0.310, below the wedge's lower bound of 0.375 — unreachable no matter how x is tuned, with the nearest point being the blue dot (clergy overshooting 31.6%). Right: the whole decision compressed into one quantity, the clergy/nobles ratio, which must fall inside [0.375, 0.667]. Note the rightmost bar — retarget clergy to +40% and its ratio of 0.434 falls back inside the band.[/I][/SIZE][/CENTER]

In the real implementation the class count is fixed at K = 4 (matching the stratum count), so M is square and the algorithm is direct:

[LIST=1]
[*]Compute [ICODE]x = M^-1 t[/ICODE] (fixed-dimension Gaussian elimination)
[*]Check that every [ICODE]x_k >= 0[/ICODE]
[*]Any negative component — unusable, fall through to approximation
[*]Otherwise adopt it
[/LIST]

Complexity O(K^3), constant at K = 4.

Corresponding to the nonnegativity condition in the feasibility test, there is a crucial distinction here: "the equation has a solution" and "the solution is usable" are two different things. Solving exactly for the population structure from section 2 gives

[CODE]x = (6.30, 5.24, -23.38)[/CODE]

The equation is solved, det != 0, numerically flawless. But the third factor is -23.38, the engine rejects negative modifiers, and so the solution exists but is unusable. This turns out to be very common in measurement. So the solver's design goal is not a 100% feasibility rate but finding the best approximation given confirmed infeasibility.

[CENTER][IMG]FIGURE_4_URL[/IMG]
[SIZE=2][I]Figure 4: same population structure. Left, the exact solve on the first three rows; the third factor is deeply negative, landing in the region the engine rejects (red shading). Right, the usable nonnegative approximation (1.93, 0.95, 0.21), at the cost of clergy missing its target by 31.6%.[/I][/SIZE][/CENTER]

[B]3.4 Approximate solving[/B]

The first step is deciding how to measure a candidate. The most direct option is the absolute residual [ICODE]||M f - t||[/ICODE], but then the strata are dimensionally incomparable — noble liquid funds can be dozens of times lower-stratum funds, so absolute error is completely dominated by the large stratum.

So we scale per row, by that row's target. For each stratum s define the relative residual:

[CODE]r_s(f) = | (M f)_s - t_s |  /  | t_s |[/CODE]

On top of that, two scalars:

[CODE]L1(f)   = ( r_nobles + r_clergy + r_burghers + r_lower ) / 4     <- mean relative deviation
Linf(f) = max( r_nobles, r_clergy, r_burghers, r_lower )        <- worst stratum[/CODE]

L1 measures overall fit; Linf measures whether any stratum got sacrificed.

Let [ICODE]f = 1[/ICODE] (all factors at 1, i.e. no correction) be the raw baseline — this is the previous version's SOL algorithm, balancing location by location — with metrics [ICODE]L1_raw[/ICODE] and [ICODE]Linf_raw[/ICODE]. A candidate f is accepted only if neither metric worsens:

[CODE]L1(f)   <= L1_raw      AND      Linf(f) <= Linf_raw[/CODE]

Fail either and the candidate is discarded without entering comparison. Failing the first means there was no optimisation at all; failing the second means a stratum that was already the worst off got worse still, and the player's experience degrades.

Here we chose an L1-dominant constraint formulation. Among candidates that pass the hard constraint, comparison against the incumbent [ICODE]f*[/ICODE] runs:

[LIST]
[*]If [ICODE]L1(f) < L1(f*) - 0.01 * L1(f*)[/ICODE] — clearly better, accept
[*]Else if [ICODE]L1(f) < L1(f*) + 0.01 * L1(f*)[/ICODE] and [ICODE]Linf(f) < Linf(f*)[/ICODE] — tied on L1 but a better worst row, accept
[*]Otherwise reject
[/LIST]

So plus/minus 1% on L1 forms a tolerance band: inside it, treat as a tie and switch to asking whose worst stratum is lighter. Overall fit stays primary, without randomly picking between two near-identical L1 candidates and dropping a stratum in the process. The 1% is obviously a tunable number, but I did not spend much time on it because the effect is small.

[ICODE]M f = t[/ICODE] with [ICODE]f >= 0[/ICODE] defines a polytope. When the target is unreachable the optimum sits on the boundary — some components exactly zero, or some equations exactly satisfied. Those boundary points are the "vertices", determined by which rows participate in elimination and which columns are free.

To find those vertices the code enumerates 209 (exact row order, free column) combinations, each running one fixed-dimension Gaussian elimination to produce a candidate f, which then goes through the tests above. The best vertex becomes the approximate solution.

Note: offline replay against saves confirms these 209 combinations match full enumeration with partial pivoting exactly — no solutions missed — while shrinking the generated script by 87%.

[B]3.5 Prefilters and the overall flow[/B]

Two prefilters run before solving proper, both cheap.

The first is the size filter: any country with fewer than 5 locations goes straight to the raw-only path, skipping even classification. The reason is the pigeonhole principle — below four locations the matrix has at most 3 non-empty columns, so it cannot cover four strata anyway.

Measurement supports the trade: a single-location country's median scaled L1 only improves from 0.329 to 0.310, whereas from three locations up it is 0.371 to 0.222. Skipping them drops 70% of the exact-failed country count for 17% of the traversal.

The second is the residual filter: if all four of raw's relative residuals are already within tolerance, no correction is needed and the sweep cannot improve anything, so it is skipped. This layer matters less.

The complete flow:

[CODE]size filter ---- num_locations < 5 ----> raw-only (skip classification and solving)
        |
     5 locations or more
        v
CMF toggle check ---- both toggles for this side off ----> raw-only
        |
     at least one on
        v
raw error filter ---- all four rows within tolerance ----> skip (keep raw)
        |
     some row over
        v
exact solve (4x4 Gaussian) ---- success ----> adopt, strategy id 7
        |
      fail (singular / negative component)
        v
209-vertex sweep (hard no-worsen + L1 primary) ---- found ----> adopt, strategy id 8
        |
      nothing admissible
        v
raw_fallback (all four factors = 1, degrading to location-level balance, strategy id 0)[/CODE]

Human and AI countries take the same path; the only difference is the four CMF toggles (AI/human x exact/approximate) controlling whether each tier is enabled.

[/SPOILER]

[HR][/HR]

[SIZE=5][B]4. Solver outcomes[/B][/SIZE]

This section replays the algorithm in full against real saves at three timepoints: 1337.10, 1386.03 and 1743.04.

[B]4.1 Outcome rates[/B]

Counting where each country ends up, in the runtime's own priority order:

[TABLE]
[TR][TH]Category[/TH][TH]1337.10[/TH][TH]1386.03[/TH][TH]1743.04[/TH][/TR]
[TR][TD]prefiltered -> raw[/TD][TD]965 (67.0%)[/TD][TD]629 (59.4%)[/TD][TD]261 (44.3%)[/TD][/TR]
[TR][TD]unsolvable -> raw[/TD][TD]55 (3.8%)[/TD][TD]47 (4.4%)[/TD][TD]82 (13.9%)[/TD][/TR]
[TR][TD]approximate solve[/TD][TD]407 (28.2%)[/TD][TD]348 (32.9%)[/TD][TD]236 (40.1%)[/TD][/TR]
[TR][TD]exact solve[/TD][TD]14 (1.0%)[/TD][TD]35 (3.3%)[/TD][TD]10 (1.7%)[/TD][/TR]
[TR][TD][B]total[/B][/TD][TD][B]1,441[/B][/TD][TD][B]1,059[/B][/TD][TD][B]589[/B][/TD][/TR]
[/TABLE]

Several things worth noting:

[LIST=1]
[*]The exact solve is extremely rare, only 1.0% to 3.3%. Section 3 explained that a K=4 square system requires all four components nonnegative, and real data almost never satisfies that. Of course, since many players like to expand and hold more locations, players should expect a higher exact-solve rate than this.
[*]The approximation is the workhorse, covering 28.2% to 40.1%. Adding the exact solve, 29.2% to 41.8% of countries actually receive a correction.
[*]The unsolvable cases sit mostly among the small countries the prefilter blocks. For larger countries the unsolvable share is low — only 3.8% to 4.4% in 1337 and 1386 — and the vast majority obtain an adoptable improvement.
[/LIST]

Finally, verifying section 2's inference that more locations work better. Bucketing the countries that entered the solver by size (1337.10 save):

[TABLE]
[TR][TH]Locations[/TH][TH]Countries[/TH][TH]Exact[/TH][TH]Approx[/TH][TH]Unsolvable[/TH][TH]Corrected[/TH][/TR]
[TR][TD]5-9[/TD][TD]229[/TD][TD]3[/TD][TD]190[/TD][TD]36[/TD][TD]84.3%[/TD][/TR]
[TR][TD]10-49[/TD][TD]214[/TD][TD]6[/TD][TD]189[/TD][TD]19[/TD][TD]91.1%[/TD][/TR]
[TR][TD]50-199[/TD][TD]28[/TD][TD]2[/TD][TD]26[/TD][TD]0[/TD][TD]100.0%[/TD][/TR]
[TR][TD]200+[/TD][TD]5[/TD][TD]3[/TD][TD]2[/TD][TD]0[/TD][TD]100.0%[/TD][/TR]
[/TABLE]

The inference holds: 84.3% up to 100%, and not one country above 50 locations fails. More classes span a richer set of combinations, so the target has a better chance of landing inside reachable range.

[B]4.2 Improvement rates[/B]

The two tables below measure scaled L1 (mean relative deviation) and scaled Linf (worst-stratum deviation).

Improvement is computed [B]per country and then medianed[/B], not as median-raw divided by median-solved — the latter mixes different countries' numerators and denominators and produces a figure that corresponds to no actual country.

Scaled L1:

[TABLE]
